# THE DECISION, and it is a decision, not a default
# =================================================
# Redoubt offers NO crash reporting. Nothing about a crash is sent
# anywhere -- not to Socorro, not to Sentry, not as a Glean crash ping, and not
# on request from Remote Settings.
#
# It does NOT remove crash *diagnostics*. Every crash lib-crash sees is still
# written to the on-device database, and the crash list stays reachable two ways
# (Settings > About > Crashes, and typing "about:crashes" in the URL bar, which
# BrowserToolbarSearchMiddleware.kt:227-234 intercepts before it reaches Gecko).
# Each entry has a "Share" action -- AbstractCrashListFragment.onShareCrashClicked
# fires ACTION_SEND with DisplayableCrash.toString(), i.e. the crash UUID and the
# full stack trace as plain text -- so a user CAN attach a crash to an issue by
# hand. That is the balance the board's risk note asks for: remove the upload,
# keep the evidence.
#
# Why not keep an opt-in upload? Because there is nothing to upload to. Socorro
# is Mozilla's service, keyed by Mozilla product ids, and LibreWolf runs no crash
# collector of its own. An "opt-in" that sends LibreWolf crashes to Mozilla's
# Socorro under a Fenix product name is worse than no reporting: it is data
# egress the user did not intend, from a build Mozilla did not produce.
#
# The consequence is a support cost and it is real: docs/android/TRIAGE.md exists
# because every launch-week signal arrives as a hand-written issue. This patch
# does not reduce that cost, it confirms it. See "WHAT A USER CAN ACTUALLY
# ATTACH" below for exactly how much (and how little) the local record contains.
#
#
# WHAT --disable-crashreporter ALREADY ACHIEVED (checked, not assumed)
# ===================================================================
# assets/mozconfig.android carries --disable-crashreporter (LW-M0-03). Verified
# in the artefacts of the combined M4 build (obj-x86_64, x86_64 debug APK):
#
#   * config.status has NO 'MOZ_CRASHREPORTER' subst at all. The only match for
#     the string is 'MOZ_CRASHREPORTER_URL': 'https://crash-reports.mozilla.com',
#     which is set unconditionally and is only ever *read* by code that
#     MOZ_CRASHREPORTER gates.
#   * The generated BuildConfig for both consumers says false:
#     .../geckoview/generated/source/buildConfig/debug/org/mozilla/geckoview/BuildConfig.java:41
#       public static final boolean MOZ_CRASHREPORTER = false;
#     .../lib/crash/generated/source/buildConfig/debug/mozilla/components/lib/crash/BuildConfig.java:11
#       the same.
#   * So GeckoRuntime.java:429 startCrashHelper() early-returns, and no breakpad
#     exception handler is compiled into libxul.
#   * ChildCrashHandler.sys.mjs:68-74 returns before sending
#     "GeckoView:ChildCrashReport" unless AppConstants.MOZ_CRASHREPORTER. So a
#     CONTENT process crash never reaches the Kotlin layer as a
#     Crash.NativeCodeCrash at all.
#   * toolkit/components/crashes/moz.build:18-33 gates CrashManager.sys.mjs,
#     CrashService.sys.mjs and RemoteSettingsCrashPull.sys.mjs on
#     MOZ_CRASHREPORTER, and the built assets/omni.ja confirms the absence:
#     the only crash modules in it are ChildCrashHandler.sys.mjs,
#     CrashMonitor.sys.mjs and nsCrashMonitor.sys.mjs (a local
#     startup-crash detector that never talks to the network).
#
# What that does NOT cover, and why this patch exists: everything above is Gecko
# and GeckoView. The Fenix/android-components Kotlin layer has its own,
# completely independent crash pipeline, it is not gated on MOZ_CRASHREPORTER,
# and it was fully wired and live. It uploads *Java/Kotlin* crashes, which need
# no minidump and no breakpad -- a stack trace is enough.
#
#
# THE FOUR UPLOAD PATHS, AND WHAT HAPPENS TO EACH
# ===============================================
# 1. SOCORRO, the one the task is named for. Analytics.kt built a
#    service.socorro.MozillaSocorroService (the "new" duplicate; the old
#    service.MozillaSocorroService is still in the tree for focus-android) and
#    put it in CrashReporter.services. Its sendReport() POSTs multipart/form-data,
#    gzipped, to https://crash-reports.mozilla.com/submit -- MozillaSocorroService
#    .kt:499 in the new copy, :600 in the old -- and reports success by linking to
#    https://crash-stats.mozilla.org/report/index/<id>. Both classes are DELETED,
#    not merely unreferenced.
#    Reachable before this patch on the default settings: crashReportChoice
#    defaults to Ask (Settings.kt:608-610), so after a Java crash the next launch
#    dispatched CrashAction.Initialize -> CheckDeferred -> CheckForCrashes ->
#    ShowPrompt, the user got UnsubmittedCrashDialog, and "Send" ran
#    CrashMiddleware.sendUnsentCrashReports() -> CrashReporter.submitReport() ->
#    the POST. Selecting "Automatically send" made it silent.
# 2. SENTRY. lib-crash-sentry's SentryService was added to the same list whenever
#    BuildConfig.SENTRY_TOKEN was non-empty. In our configuration the token is
#    null, so it never initialised -- and that is exactly the "absent because
#    unconfigured" that this project does not accept as absent (cf. no-adjust's
#    ADJUST_TOKEN and no-gms's GPS_INTEGRITY_TOKEN). The :components:lib-crash-sentry
#    dependency is removed from the app, and so is `implementation libs.sentry`.
#    THE SECOND LINE IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE: with only the wrapper removed the
#    built APK still defined 1200 Lio/sentry/ classes, because the app declared
#    the SDK directly as well. Measured, both ways round; see the numbers below.
# 3. THE GLEAN CRASH PING. GleanCrashReporterService sat in
#    CrashReporter.telemetryServices. patches/android/no-glean.patch (LW-M4-01)
#    removed Glean.initialize() and the in-tree HTTP transport but left THIS
#    service wired -- it is a crash-reporting service, so it belongs to this task.
#    Its class is deleted and telemetryServices is empty. (With Glean
#    uninitialised it could not have uploaded; it was still assembling crash
#    metadata and writing it to a file on every crash.)
# 4. THE REMOTE SETTINGS CRASH PULL. This is the one that is easy to miss: it is
#    not a path by which we send crashes, it is a path by which Mozilla ASKS a
#    particular user to send particular pending minidumps. GeckoProvider.kt set
#    geckoRuntime.crashPullDelegate, which makes GeckoView emit
#    "GeckoView:CrashPullController.Delegate:Attached"; GeckoViewStartup.sys.mjs
#    :450-462 answers that by starting RemoteSettingsCrashPull, which subscribes
#    to the "crash-reports-ondemand" collection and, on a match, dispatches
#    CrashAction.CheckDeferred with a list of crash IDs straight into
#    CrashMiddleware -- i.e. into path 1. The delegate assignment is removed.
#    It could not have fired in this build (see the omni.ja finding above), but
#    "cannot fire because a module happens not to be packaged" is a coincidence,
#    not a decision.
#
#
