#
#
# THE ONE REMAINING "crash-reports.mozilla.com" STRING, and why it is dead
# =======================================================================
# It is CrashHandler.java:43-45 in GeckoView:
#   DEFAULT_SERVER_URL = "https://crash-reports.mozilla.com/submit?id=%1$s&..."
# Do not "fix" it by editing the string; trace it instead. It is read only by
# CrashHandler.getServerUrl() (:341-347), which is called only by
# CrashHandler.reportException() (:513), which writes the URL into a local .extra
# file next to a locally written .dmp and then calls launchCrashReporter() -- it
# performs no network I/O itself. Whether that ever runs:
#
#   * reportException() is overridden in createDefaultCrashHandler() (:586-612)
#     to return false unless BuildConfig.MOZ_CRASHREPORTER && MOZILLA_OFFICIAL,
#     and MOZ_CRASHREPORTER is false in this build (verified above), and
#   * `grep -rn "createDefaultCrashHandler\|new CrashHandler("` over mobile/
#     returns only the definition itself. Nothing in the tree ever constructs a
#     CrashHandler. It is public GeckoView API for third-party embedders.
#
# The only CrashHandler code Fenix reaches is the package-private static
# launchCrashReporter(handlerService, context, bundle) called from
# GeckoRuntime.java:363-365 on a "GeckoView:ChildCrashReport" event, which
# ChildCrashHandler.sys.mjs never sends without MOZ_CRASHREPORTER, and which
# takes its paths from the event -- never from DEFAULT_SERVER_URL. Left in place
# deliberately: patching a dead constant in a public GeckoView class would add a
# tree file to this patch (and an apilint surface) for no behavioural change.
#
#
# THE UI HAD TO CHANGE TOO, or the build would lie to the user
# ===========================================================
# Removing the services is not enough: three visible surfaces offered to send a
# report. A button that promises to send a crash report and then sends nothing is
# a worse bug than the upload was.
#
#   * SettingsCrashReportCache.getReportOption() now returns Never
#     unconditionally (and setReportOption is a no-op). This is the single choke
#     point: CrashMiddleware.invoke()'s Initialize handler returns immediately on
#     Never, so UnsubmittedCrashDialog is never shown, submitReport is never
#     called, and -- importantly -- no crash is left marked "unsent" to be
#     re-offered at every subsequent launch. Ignoring the stored preference is
#     deliberate: a profile that had "Automatically send" set must not keep it.
#   * DataChoicesScreen.kt no longer adds the CRASH_REPORTS section (the
#     Ask / Automatically send / Never radio group) to the Data Choices screen,
#     and drops its settings-search entry so search cannot navigate to a section
#     that is not rendered. The composable itself is left in place so the
#     `when (section)` branch still references it (no dead-code warning) and so a
#     rebase sees the smallest possible diff.
#   * view_crash_reporter.xml drops the "Send crash report to Mozilla" CheckBox
#     from the tab-crash panel -- the panel a user DOES still see, because a
#     content-process crash still marks the tab crashed via
#     ContentDelegate.onCrash regardless of MOZ_CRASHREPORTER. CrashContentView
#     passes false to handleCloseAndRestore/handleCloseAndRemove. Nothing else in
#     that layout constrained to the checkbox; both buttons are anchored to the
#     parent bottom.
#   * StartupCrashActivity starts its store in UiState.Finished instead of
#     UiState.Idle. Idle is the state that renders the "Send crash report" /
#     "No thanks" pair; Finished renders only "Reopen". The startup-crash flow
#     otherwise works exactly as documented in fenix/startupCrash/README.md.
#   * PrivacyPreferencesRepository.setPreference() no longer writes
#     Settings.crashReportChoice for PreferenceType.CrashReporting. This is the
#     onboarding "Manage privacy preferences" dialog behind the ToS screen. Its UI
#     is already unreachable (patches/android/no-onboarding.patch, LW-M4-10), but
#     the repository still compiles and still ran, and leaving the write in place
#     would let a consent be *recorded* that nothing can honour. FOUND BY THE UNIT
#     TESTS, NOT BY READING THE CODE -- two DefaultPrivacyPreferencesRepositoryTest
#     cases went red on the crashReportOption() change and that is how this fifth
#     surface surfaced at all. Exactly the casualty class the working agreement
#     warns about, caught this time because the tests were run.
#   * strings.xml gains librewolf_startup_crash_body, used instead of
#     startup_crash_body, whose second paragraph says "Sending a crash report
#     helps us diagnose and fix problems with the browser." A NEW key rather than
#     an edit to the existing one, on purpose: Fenix's 130 values-*/strings.xml
#     locale files are synced from mozilla-l10n by Mozilla automation and are not
#     ours to edit, so editing startup_crash_body would leave every non-English
#     locale asserting that a report is being sent, while an untranslated new key
#     falls back to the English text everywhere. That fallback IS the known
#     limitation: this one paragraph is English-only until LW-M4-07/LW-M5-03
#     settle how LibreWolf overrides Android strings.
#
# Also set: BuildConfig.CRASH_REPORTING is now false on every channel, not just
# debug. Settings.isCrashReportEnabledInBuild reads it (Settings.kt:181-182) and
# it is the second gate on CrashReporterController's submit path.
#
#
# lib-crash's `require` HAD to be relaxed
# =======================================
# CrashReporter's init block is
#   require(services.isNotEmpty() || telemetryServices.isNotEmpty())
# so "no crash reporting services" is not an expressible configuration upstream.
# Passing two empty lists without touching it would throw at construction and
# take the LOCAL crash record down with it, since CrashReporter.onCrash() is what
# writes the database row. The require is replaced by a comment. Everything else
# in CrashReporter.kt is untouched: onCrash() still records, breadcrumbs still
# accumulate, submitReport()/submitCaughtException() still exist and now iterate
# empty lists. No test asserted the require message
# (`grep -rn "No crash reporter services defined"` -> the source line only).
#
#
# WHAT A USER CAN ACTUALLY ATTACH -- state this plainly, it is a support cost
# ==========================================================================
# Kept, and populated:
#   * Java/Kotlin uncaught exceptions in the Fenix processes. lib-crash's
#     ExceptionHandler catches them, onCrash() writes uuid + full stack trace +
#     runtime tags to the Room database, and about:crashes lists them with a
#     Share action. This is the only crash class that lands in the crash list.
#   * logcat, for everything. `adb logcat` from a computer is the documented
#     route in docs/android/TRIAGE.md and remains the only way to get anything
#     out of a native crash.
# NOT captured, and nothing this patch could do would change it:
#   * Native crashes -- content, GPU or parent process. With
#     --disable-crashreporter there is no breakpad, so no minidump is written and
#     ChildCrashHandler.sys.mjs never notifies the app. A content crash produces
#     the in-app "can't load that page" panel plus an Android tombstone in
#     logcat, and NO entry in the crash list. This is a property of the mozconfig
#     (LW-M0-03 / LW-M5-03), not of this patch, and it was already true before it.
#   * ANRs. Never were in lib-crash's scope.
# So: a user reporting a native crash has to capture logcat, or the report is
# "the tab died". Someone has to accept that; this header is where it is written
# down.
#
#
# DELIBERATE LEFTOVERS
# ====================
#   * The :components:lib-crash-sentry MODULE still exists in the tree. No
